WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.180
Safe command execution is not finished when a process starts.

2
00:00:04.280 --> 00:00:07.560
The script also has to decide what the result means.

3
00:00:07.660 --> 00:00:16.780
Commands can fail because files are missing, permissions changed, tools are unavailable, network calls timed out, or preconditions were never true.

4
00:00:16.880 --> 00:00:22.960
A script that ignores those results can report success while leaving a system only partially updated.

5
00:00:23.240 --> 00:00:25.880
Exit status is the basic signal.

6
00:00:25.980 --> 00:00:33.820
Commands report whether they succeeded according to their own rules, and scripts should check that signal when the result affects later work.

7
00:00:33.920 --> 00:00:38.400
Some non-success outcomes may be expected, but they should be handled deliberately.

8
00:00:38.500 --> 00:00:44.260
The unsafe pattern is to let failure drift forward until a later step creates confusing output.

9
00:00:44.540 --> 00:00:50.300
Pipelines need special attention because the visible result of a pipeline may not tell the whole story.

10
00:00:50.400 --> 00:00:56.080
A later command can succeed after an earlier command failed, depending on shell options and script structure.

11
00:00:56.180 --> 00:01:04.260
Defensive scripts make pipeline behavior explicit where it matters and avoid treating a formatted final output as proof that every upstream step worked.

12
00:01:04.540 --> 00:01:08.140
Precondition checks help stop unsafe runs early.

13
00:01:08.240 --> 00:01:16.400
If a required file, directory, account, tool, or configuration value is missing, the script should say so before it begins changing state.

14
00:01:16.500 --> 00:01:24.020
This is especially important for unattended jobs, where a clear failure message may be the only clue an operator sees.

15
00:01:24.300 --> 00:01:28.460
Timeouts and retry limits are part of safe failure.

16
00:01:28.560 --> 00:01:33.120
A command that hangs forever can block a deployment or maintenance window.

17
00:01:33.220 --> 00:01:38.980
A retry loop with no limit can create operational noise or repeated partial work.

18
00:01:39.080 --> 00:01:46.200
Good automation sets boundaries, stops when assumptions are not met, and gives the next human enough information to decide what to do.

19
00:01:46.480 --> 00:01:48.800
The goal is not to make scripts fragile.

20
00:01:48.900 --> 00:01:51.060
The goal is to make failures honest.

21
00:01:51.160 --> 00:01:58.920
A safe script distinguishes expected conditions from real errors, avoids misleading success, and stays rerunnable where practical.

22
00:01:59.020 --> 00:02:03.900
That turns command execution from a hopeful sequence of steps into a controlled workflow.
